  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Weak cold front today, then a stronger system \nmoves across PA on Thursday night and Friday\n\nLow pressure over NY this morning will move east and drag a \ncold front down from the north and across all of PA by 00Z. \nSHRA and TSRA are already over nrn OH and will reach the western\ncounties in the next 3-4 hrs. The risk for thunder is there, \nbut low, as the best low level jet winds are south of the state,\nand some CIN is expected to remain for much of the day. Thus, \nconvection is more likely to be elevated (not rooted at the \nsfc). Bulk shear is progged >40kt at JST, but tops are only \nexpected to be 20-25kft. Pretty rare that we get any severe \ngusts with CAPE only in the 200-500J/kg range and no favorable\nthermodynamic environment to allow winds to accelerate \ncompletely down to the sfc. \n\nQPF for today is low with just 0.50\" as the top end in the \nLaurels. Any TSRA should be moving along steadily. So, we should\nhave no risk for flooding rains today.\n\nThe next storm system will be driven by a sharp upper trough\nwith a strong sfc high behind it. PWAT does increase to 1.8\" in\nthe S. The showers and any storms will be moving very quickly\nThursday night and Friday. 1hr FFG is currently 0.8\" and \nhigher - but could decrease very slightly due to any rain today. \nStorm total QPF in the 0.1-0.2\" range in the SC-SE and near \n0.75\" in the NW (where FFG is higher) should not lead to \nflooding. \n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Windy end to the week with first flakes in the \nhigher terrain \n\nWind gusts 30-45 mph are forecast on the backside of the cold\nfront and will deliver much colder air downwind of the Great \nLakes this weekend. Latest NBM probs hold only 10pct chc for \nadvy gusts - and only on the very highest elevations. JST BUFKIT\nprofile from GFS does nudge 60KT winds down to 5kft AGL. So, it\nwill be windy, and worth monitoring for an advy at some point -\nbut only for the highest elevations - probably not for 90pct of\nthe CWA.\n\nThe shot of colder air will allow for rain to mix or changeover\nto snow showers Friday night into Saturday morning across the \nhigher terrain along the Allegheny Mtns. Any minor accum (<0.5\")\nwould have to occur overnight and be limited to non paved \nsurfaces.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Cold weather may end the growing season for many\nplaces Sun morning. \n\nBig sfc high moves in for the weekend, supplying dry but cold\nweather for Sat and Sun. Sat will hold the coldest daytime \ntemps with temps getting just above 40F in the NE. Sat AM could\nbe sub-freezing for the nrn tier. So far, only McKean Co has\nbeen taken off the list of zones to need any further\nfrost/freeze products. But, we could add to that list Sat AM -\nand especially on Sun AM. Sunday morning lows expected to be in\nthe upper 20s to mid 30s may kill the growing season for a \nsignificant portion of the CWA. We'll be considering appropriate\nfrost/freeze products, but it is still too early to be that \nconfident on details. Pretty cold again Monday AM, but not \nquite as cold - and some of the area may not need any more \nfrost/freeze products by then.",
